.TH FKJOIN 3 2026-09-24 "fkstring" "fkstring Library Manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
fkjoin \- concatenate an array of fkstrings with a separator
.SH LIBRARY
fkstring library
.RI ( libfkstring ,\~ \-lfkstring )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fkstring.h>
.PP
.BI "fkstring *fkjoin(fkstring **" arr ", const char *" sep );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fkjoin ()
concatenates the elements of the NULL-terminated array
.I arr
into a new
.BR fkstring ,
placing the NUL-terminated string
.I sep
between each pair of consecutive elements (not before the first or after
the last).
A NULL
.I sep
is treated as an empty string.
.PP
Empty elements still get their separators, so
.BR fkjoin ()
is the inverse of
.BR fksplit (3):
joining the parts of
.I s
split on
.I c
with a
.I sep
of just
.I c
reproduces
.IR s .
Embedded NUL bytes in the elements are preserved.
.PP
The total length is computed first, so the result is allocated once.
.I arr
and its elements are not modified.
.SH RETURN VALUE
A new
.BR fkstring ,
which is empty if
.I arr
has no elements.
.BR fkjoin ()
returns NULL if
.I arr
is NULL.
.SH ERRORS
.BR fkjoin ()
does not return on allocation failure, nor when the total length
overflows
.BR size_t ;
see
.BR fkstring (3).
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
fkstring **parts = fksplit(s, \(aq,\(aq);
fkstring *csv = fkjoin(parts, ", ");   /* "a,b,c" \-> "a, b, c" */
\&
fkarraydestroy(parts);
fkstrdestroy(csv);
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fkstring (3),
.BR fksplit (3),
.BR fkstrcat (3)
